==English==
{{was wotd|2017|March|27}}
[[File:Aristoteles Louvre.jpg|thumb|A marble sculpture of the Greek [[philosopher]] and [[scientist]] {{w|Aristotle}} (384–322 [[BCE|{{smallcaps|b.c.e.}}]], whose work ''{{w|Prior Analytics}}'' contained an early discussion of the [[logical]] [[fallacy]] of begging the question. The sculpture, a copy of a lost 1st- or 2nd-century [[bronze]] statue by {{w|Lysippos}}, is in the collection of the [[Louvre]] in [[Paris]], [[France]].]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈbɛɡɪŋ ðə ˈkwɛst͡ʃən/|/-jən/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|beg|ging the ques|tion|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# A [[logical]] [[fallacy]] in which a [[premise]] of an [[argument]] contains a [[direct]] or [[indirect]] [[assumption]] that the [[conclusion]] is true; offering a [[circular argument]]; [[circular reasoning]].
#: {{ux|en|It is an instance of '''begging the question''' to argue that God can only do good deeds because God is good.}}

===Verb===
{{head|en|present participle}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} {{present participle of|beg the question|lang=en|nocat=1}}.

====Usage notes====
In common usage, ''begging the question'' has recently become synonymous with “raising the question”; this usage is often [[proscribe]]d.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|circular argument}}, {{l|en|circular reasoning}} {{qualifier|casual usage}}
* {{l|en|hysteron proteron}}
* {{l|en|petitio principii}}

